@daniel_271828 Like, if a policeman is on patrol, sees someone of race X being harassed or threatened and doesn't help them, but would if they were of race Y, that's obviously unacceptable. But it's unclear if that's happening here.
More pragmatically, police officers who don't have relevant and strong political views will generally do a better job in these situations, and letting people express a preference lets you deploy better suited officers. Even if someone is making a sincere effort to be professional, it's just easier if you aren't very distracted.
If ALL officers express a preference against and no one is assigned, that starts to be concerning (but I doubt that's what's going on)

